        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "47d4dd4e-7df8-56d7-ba42-faa8368645d8",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: William Calley Jr., J. Robert Elliott\n- Events: My Lai Massacre, My Lai investigation\n- Organizations: C Company, United States House of Representatives\n- Roles: platoon leader\n\nFacts:\n- After the My Lai Massacre, twenty-six soldiers were charged with criminal offenses.\n- William Calley Jr. was a platoon leader in C Company during the My Lai Massacre.\n- William Calley Jr. was the only charged soldier convicted for the My Lai Massacre.\n- William Calley Jr. was found guilty of killing 22 villagers and was originally sentenced to life imprisonment.\n- William Calley Jr. served only three and a half years under house arrest.\n- William Calley Jr. was later released after federal judge J. Robert Elliott ruled that Calley\u0019s trial had been prejudiced by pre-trial publicity, denial of subpoenas for certain defense witnesses, refusal by the United States House of Representatives to release executive-session testimony from its My Lai investigation, and inadequate notice of the charges.\n- William Calley Jr. was not technically exonerated.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "b7b3ad2c-b944-5d38-b1c9-dc2af8b9ec4e",
        "text": "Page: My Lai Massacre. Sentence: Twenty-six soldiers were charged with criminal offenses, but only Lieutenant William Calley Jr., a platoon leader in C Company, was convicted.\nPage: My Lai Massacre. Sentence: Found guilty of killing 22 villagers, he was originally given a life sentence, but served only three and a half years under house arrest.\nPage: William Calley. Sentence: While not technically exonerated, after three and a half years of house arrest, Calley was released after a ruling by federal judge J. Robert Elliott who found that Calley's trial had been prejudiced by pre-trial publicity, denial of subpoenas of certain defense witnesses, refusal of the United States House of Representatives to release testimony taken in executive session of its My Lai investigation, and inadequate notice of the charges.",
        "type": "DocumentChunk"
